1
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:05,790
More than 30 years later, in 1704,

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:09,130
in London, Newton publishes a book called
Opticks.

3
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:12,190
And on page 90, he gives his answer.

4
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:15,680
It's a sentence that's easy to read past.

5
00:00:16,193 --> 00:00:19,080
"For the rays to speak properly are not
coloured."

6
00:00:19,666 --> 00:00:23,430
In plain words, strictly speaking, the
rays have no colour.

7
00:00:24,386 --> 00:00:26,360
All they have, he goes on,

8
00:00:26,466 --> 00:00:29,176
is "a certain power and disposition to
stir

9
00:00:29,226 --> 00:00:31,860
up a sensation of this or that colour".

10
00:00:32,806 --> 00:00:35,430
That sounds like it clashes with the
prism.

11
00:00:35,959 --> 00:00:36,959
It doesn't.

12
00:00:37,412 --> 00:00:39,980
What's in sunlight is many kinds of ray.

13
00:00:40,545 --> 00:00:43,100
The light coming off that geranium isn't
pink.

14
00:00:43,678 --> 00:00:46,060
Each ray only has a kind of push.

15
00:00:46,118 --> 00:00:49,900
And the pink is what happens when that
push reaches whoever is looking.

16
00:00:50,898 --> 00:00:54,750
So, on Newton's view, colour isn't really
in the flower.

17
00:00:55,271 --> 00:00:56,890
It isn't in the light either.

18
00:00:57,444 --> 00:01:00,460
It's something that happens where the
light meets a person.

19
00:01:01,464 --> 00:01:04,370
But a geranium and a leaf still look
different.

20
00:01:04,937 --> 00:01:06,920
Newton has an answer for that, too.

21
00:01:07,550 --> 00:01:08,980
In his letter from Cambridge,

22
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:10,840
he says things get their colours by
sending

23
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:13,310
back some kinds of light more than others.

24
00:01:13,923 --> 00:01:15,960
A geranium sends back one mix.

25
00:01:16,516 --> 00:01:18,240
A leaf sends back another.

26
00:01:18,789 --> 00:01:20,619
Neither one is painting the light.

27
00:01:20,669 --> 00:01:22,450
Each is choosing from it.

28
00:01:23,449 --> 00:01:25,170
That also explains the candle.

29
00:01:25,702 --> 00:01:29,720
Candlelight is much yellower than
daylight, with far less blue in it.

30
00:01:30,394 --> 00:01:34,330
So by candle, the geranium has a different
mix of rays to choose from.

31
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:38,260
And to most people, that change hardly
matters.

32
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,070
Their eyes still call it pink.

33
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:43,260
Dalton's eyes don't.

34
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:46,980
Newton also gives us the rainbow we learn
at school.

35
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:49,770
He splits his band of colours into seven.

36
00:01:50,406 --> 00:01:54,930
Red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo
and violet.

37
00:01:55,519 --> 00:02:00,010
And he spaces them in the same proportions
as the seven steps of a musical scale.

38
00:02:00,959 --> 00:02:03,090
Seven notes, seven colours.

39
00:02:03,512 --> 00:02:04,512
It's tidy.

40
00:02:04,965 --> 00:02:06,920
Possibly a little too tidy.

41
00:02:07,438 --> 00:02:10,220
People have been arguing about indigo ever
since.

42
00:02:11,138 --> 00:02:13,350
Here's why all this matters for Dalton.

43
00:02:13,871 --> 00:02:15,530
He knows this science.

44
00:02:16,044 --> 00:02:20,180
He says he's "pretty well acquainted with
the theory of light and colours".

45
00:02:20,697 --> 00:02:23,910
And on Newton's terms, his idea makes good
sense.

46
00:02:24,837 --> 00:02:28,650
Colour is what happens when certain rays
reach the back of the eye.

47
00:02:29,230 --> 00:02:32,800
So a tinted jelly in front of it changes
which rays arrive.

48
00:02:33,323 --> 00:02:35,760
Change the rays, and you change the
colour.

49
00:02:36,663 --> 00:02:40,710
But a guess about the inside of an eye can
only be tested one way.

50
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:43,690
Someone has to open it.
